From 7bc4b9c596891c6e0b0e2063cf7e9af0932701cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tengzhen <tengzhen.xiong@tuya.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 09:26:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] alientek rk3506: bound the /tmp and /var/log tmpfs

Both are tmpfs, i.e. RAM, and a tmpfs with no size= defaults to half of
physical memory. Nothing on this board rotates or truncates its logs:
S98wukong_ai appends the app's entire stdout+stderr to
/var/log/wukong_ai.log with >> and never rolls it, ble_boot.log grows per
boot, and TKL_LOG writes /tmp/tkl_wifi.log in addition to stdout so every
Wi-Fi line is stored twice. A device churning through reconnects can hand
half its RAM to log text, and the failure is not a clean OOM: once tmpfs
is full, /run pid files and the ble_ready flag files start failing too.

Cap /tmp at 16M and /var/log at 8M. This does not stop the growth, it
bounds the blast radius - a runaway log fails its own writes with ENOSPC
instead of starving the rest of the system.

Sized against what actually lands there: /tmp otherwise holds only small
TKL files (hostapd/dnsmasq conf, pid, leases), and there is no OTA
staging to accommodate because tkl_ota.c returns OPRT_NOT_SUPPORTED on
this port.

The file overrides package/skeleton-init-sysv/skeleton/etc/fstab, which
is shared by every board here, so it is a board overlay rather than a
patch there. It carries the skeleton's lines only. /, proc, devtmpfs,
devpts, /dev/shm, sysfs, configfs, debugfs, pstore, /oem and /userdata
are all deleted and re-appended by post-fstab.sh, which runs as a
post-rootfs hook after this overlay is applied; listing them here would
just be duplicates it rewrites. That hook does not touch /tmp, /run or
/var/log, which is what lets these caps survive - verified against the
generated target/etc/fstab.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../atk-dlrk3506/fs-overlay/etc/fstab         | 37 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 board/alientek/atk-dlrk3506/fs-overlay/etc/fstab

diff --git a/board/alientek/atk-dlrk3506/fs-overlay/etc/fstab b/board/alientek/atk-dlrk3506/fs-overlay/etc/fstab
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..366f41a2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/board/alientek/atk-dlrk3506/fs-overlay/etc/fstab
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+# Board fstab for ATK-DLRK3506B.
+#
+# Overrides package/skeleton-init-sysv/skeleton/etc/fstab (shared by every board
+# in this tree, hence a copy here rather than a patch there). Content is the
+# skeleton verbatim apart from size= on /tmp and /var/log.
+#
+# Deliberately does NOT list /, proc, devtmpfs, devpts, /dev/shm, sysfs,
+# configfs, debugfs, pstore, /oem or /userdata. device/rockchip/common/scripts/
+# post-fstab.sh runs as a post-rootfs hook AFTER this overlay is applied and
+# deletes + re-appends every one of those from the partition layout. Repeating
+# them here would just be stale duplicates the hook rewrites anyway. It does not
+# touch /tmp, /run or /var/log, which is why the caps below survive.
+#
+# Why cap: both are tmpfs, i.e. RAM, and a tmpfs with no size= defaults to half
+# of physical memory. Nothing on this board rotates or truncates its logs:
+#   /var/log/wukong_ai.log  S98wukong_ai appends the app's whole stdout+stderr
+#                           with >>, forever, across restarts
+#   /var/log/ble_boot.log   S39/S41/S98 boot trace
+#   /tmp/tkl_wifi.log       TKL_LOG writes here as well as to stdout, so every
+#                           Wi-Fi line costs RAM twice
+# Uncapped, a device churning through reconnects can hand half its RAM to log
+# text. The cap does not stop the growth, it bounds the blast radius: a runaway
+# log fails its own writes with ENOSPC instead of starving the system. Raise
+# them if you need a longer field trace.
+#
+# /tmp otherwise holds only small TKL files (hostapd/dnsmasq conf, pid, leases).
+# No OTA staging to size around - tkl_ota.c returns OPRT_NOT_SUPPORTED here.
+#
+# <file system>	<mount pt>	<type>	<options>	<dump>	<pass>
+/dev/root	/		ext2	rw,noauto	0	1
+proc		/proc		proc	defaults	0	0
+devpts		/dev/pts	devpts	defaults,gid=5,mode=620,ptmxmode=0666	0	0
+tmpfs		/dev/shm	tmpfs	mode=0777	0	0
+tmpfs		/tmp		tmpfs	mode=1777,size=16M	0	0
+tmpfs		/run		tmpfs	mode=0755,nosuid,nodev	0	0
+tmpfs		/var/log	tmpfs	mode=0755,nosuid,nodev,size=8M	0	0
+sysfs		/sys		sysfs	defaults	0	0
-- 
2.25.1

